🚽 Test case: takes_char() declares a plain STDROT_CHAR parameter. A
🚽 bare char identifier (not an array, not a struct field, not an
🚽 expression) must still work correctly -- this is the positive
🚽 counterpart to semantic_error_native_char_array_access.brainrot and
🚽 semantic_error_native_char_struct_access.brainrot. Also exercises the
🚽 deprecated slorp(c) write-back path immediately beforehand, which
🚽 previously corrupted `c`'s own var_type via set_int_variable() instead
🚽 of set_char_variable(), silently turning a later `takes_char(c)` into
🚽 a spurious runtime ABI-violation abort.
skibidi main {
    yap c;
    slorp(c);
    rizz r = takes_char(c);
    yapping("%d", r);
    bussin 0;
}
